# Microsoft TLS root certificates
#
# login.microsoftonline.com is served with a chain ending at "Microsoft
# TLS RSA Root G2", which is in neither certifi's bundle nor Debian's —
# they carry Microsoft's 2017 roots and DigiCert's, not this one.
#
# It normally still verifies, because the server also sends a copy of
# that root cross-signed by DigiCert Global Root G2, which bridges to a
# root both bundles do have. When a node omits that bridge certificate,
# the chain terminates at an unknown root and OpenSSL reports "unable to
# get local issuer certificate" — which is why token validation failed
# on some replicas and not others, with nothing in the application
# changed.
#
# Trusting these roots directly removes the dependency on what any
# particular node chooses to send.
#
# Downloaded from Microsoft's PKI distribution point:
#   https://www.microsoft.com/pkiops/certs/Microsoft%20TLS%20RSA%20Root%20G2.crt
#   https://www.microsoft.com/pkiops/certs/Microsoft%20TLS%20ECC%20Root%20G2.crt
# Both self-signed, valid to April 2040.
